{"type": "response", "step": 4, "latency_s": 6.725145, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cgm6GsuerKVc3eiLbDESs", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't delete anything. I couldn't find any bad rows for June 13 in `db.events`, so there was nothing clear to remove.\n\n**What I checked:**\n- **Rows per day:** Every day from June 1 to June 12 has 24 events, one per hour. (June 1 has 23 because the data starts at 01:00.) June 13 has only 13 events, from 00:00 to 12:00.\n- **The June 13 rows themselves look normal:** event_ids 288–300 run in order with no gaps, event types follow the usual view/click/purchase pattern, customer_ids look normal, and none of the fields are empty.\n- **Whole table:** 300 rows, event_ids 1–300 with no repeats and no empty fields. The last event in the table is 2026-06-13 12:00.\n- **Change history:** every saved version of the table only added rows. Nothing was deleted or overwritten.\n\n**Most likely cause:** The data stops at noon on June 13, so that day only has about half its usual events. The dashboard is probably showing a drop of roughly 50% on the last day. That points to missing data (a load that cut off early or hasn't finished), not wrong data. Deleting the 13 real rows would remove valid events and make the dip worse.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- Check whether the job that loads events stopped after 12:00 on June 13, and reload the rest of the day if it did.\n- If you'd rather not show the half day for now, the dashboard could be filtered to full days only.\n- If you saw something other than a drop, such as a spike or odd values, tell me what it looked like and I'll look again.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation